Subject: [PW3] Applications in /data are compiled again as installed after a system update

A new system brings a new boot image, and the compiled code of the applications
in /data no longer matches it. AOSP compiles them again on the first boot with
pm.dexopt.boot ("verify": no compiled code), leaves the rest to the job that
runs when idle and charging, and skips applications not used in the week
before. On this CPU a large application then starts in the interpreter (WeRead
without compiled code: over 2 minutes and "isn't responding", pw3.mk).

Applications whose code is in /data (installed there, or system applications
updated there) are compiled with the filter they were installed with
(pm.dexopt.install), whether used lately or not. System applications keep
pm.dexopt.boot: their code in /system was compiled for the new boot image.
They are looked at on every boot, so a boot cut short while compiling is
finished by the next one; code that still matches is skipped at once.

The first boot after an update takes as long as compiling them (WeRead: about
1410 s, Test V35); the installer says so before starting it.

--- a/services/core/java/com/android/server/pm/PackageManagerService.java
+++ b/services/core/java/com/android/server/pm/PackageManagerService.java
@@ -9812,13 +9812,27 @@
         // We need to re-extract after a pruned cache, as AoT-ed files will be out of date.
         boolean causePrunedCache = VMRuntime.didPruneDalvikCache();
 
-        if (!causeUpgrade && !causeFirstBoot && !causePrunedCache) {
-            return;
-        }
-
         List<PackageParser.Package> pkgs;
         synchronized (mPackages) {
             pkgs = PackageManagerServiceUtils.getPackagesForDexopt(mPackages.values(), this);
+            // PW3: every application whose code is in /data, also one not used in the week
+            // before (getPackagesForDexopt leaves those out): it would otherwise start in
+            // the interpreter the next time it is used.
+            for (PackageParser.Package pkg : mPackages.values()) {
+                if (isCodeInData(pkg) && !pkgs.contains(pkg)) {
+                    pkgs.add(pkg);
+                }
+            }
+        }
+        if (!causeUpgrade && !causeFirstBoot && !causePrunedCache) {
+            // PW3: applications whose code is in /data are looked at on every boot, not
+            // only on the one that first sees a new system: if that boot was cut short
+            // while compiling, the next one finishes the work. Compiled code that still
+            // matches the boot image is skipped at once.
+            pkgs.removeIf(pkg -> !isCodeInData(pkg));
+            if (pkgs.isEmpty()) {
+                return;
+            }
         }
 
         final long startTime = System.nanoTime();
@@ -9949,6 +9963,16 @@
             }
 
             String pkgCompilerFilter = compilerFilter;
+            if (isCodeInData(pkg)) {
+                // PW3: a new system brings a new boot image, and the compiled code of the
+                // applications in /data no longer matches it. AOSP compiles them again with
+                // pm.dexopt.boot ("verify": no compiled code) and leaves the rest to the
+                // idle-and-charging job, so the next start of a large application runs in
+                // the interpreter (WeRead: over 2 minutes, "isn't responding"). They get the
+                // filter they were installed with. System applications keep pm.dexopt.boot:
+                // their code in /system was compiled for this boot image.
+                pkgCompilerFilter = getCompilerFilterForReason(REASON_INSTALL);
+            }
             if (useProfileForDexopt) {
                 // Use background dexopt mode to try and use the profile. Note that this does not
                 // guarantee usage of the profile.
@@ -19220,6 +19244,12 @@
         return (ps.pkgFlags & ApplicationInfo.FLAG_EXTERNAL_STORAGE) != 0;
     }
 
+    /** PW3: whether a package's code is in /data (installed there, or a system app updated there). */
+    private static boolean isCodeInData(PackageParser.Package pkg) {
+        return !isSystemApp(pkg)
+                || (pkg.applicationInfo.flags & ApplicationInfo.FLAG_UPDATED_SYSTEM_APP) != 0;
+    }
+
     private static boolean isSystemApp(PackageParser.Package pkg) {
         return (pkg.applicationInfo.flags & ApplicationInfo.FLAG_SYSTEM) != 0;
     }
